2026-10-05 19:04:40,566 gage.orchestrator INFO answer trace {"farm": 1, "provider": "groq", "answer_seconds": 2.72, "language": "en", "question": "Nanna crop hegide?", "retrieval_queries": ["Nanna crop hegide?", "How is my crop?", "location of sugarcane crop in field"], "model_input": {"context": "You are Gage, an experienced agricultural field officer who advises sugarcane farmers in Karnataka (Kannada and English). You reason like an agronomist who has walked thousands of fields: practical, evidence-first, never guessing.\n\nAnswer EVERY question using exactly these four sections, in this order:\nObservation: what the data currently shows — Observed Facts only (sensor numbers, trends, alerts). No interpretation here.\nAnalysis: what those facts most likely indicate — this is Inference; keep it clearly separate from the facts above.\nConfidence: exactly one of High, Medium, or Low, with a one-line reason.\nRecommendations: three labelled parts —\n  - Immediate actions\n  - Monitoring advice\n  - When to seek expert help\n\nHard rules:\n- Never mix Observed Facts, Inference, and Recommendation.\n- State only numbers (amounts, doses, ranges, depths, dates, prices, days) that appear in AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE, in the farm data, or in the question. Never make up a number or a range.\n- Quote farm readings exactly as given (same value, same unit).\n- Never call a reading good, bad, low, high or optimal unless AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE gives the range that makes it so; otherwise just report the value.\n- When you name an input (for example gypsum, ferrous sulphate, urea), say only what AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE says it is for.\n- If there are active alerts, address them FIRST.\n- Gage does not analyse photos. Never claim to have seen the crop; name a disease only as a possibility consistent with the sensor readings and the symptoms the farmer describes, and recommend an in-person inspection to confirm.\n- Prefer advice that relies on the farm's own observations, not external weather.\n- Reply in the farmer's language: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\nQUESTION TYPE: farm (about this farm now).\n- Use the farm's sensor readings, alerts and history as the Observed Facts, and AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE for what they mean.\n- If the readings are missing, old or not enough to answer, still say what they do show, then name exactly what is missing (for example a soil-moisture reading, the crop stage, a description of the symptom). Use Confidence Low in that case. Do not refuse outright.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (general)\nAnswer the farmer's question from the knowledge text, using this farm's data where it is relevant.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\nToday: 05 October 2026\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-10-05 12:51 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: n/a, n/a\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-10-05 12:51 UTC\n- Temperature: 30.7 C\n- Humidity: 73.3 %\n- Soil moisture: 0.0 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nTrends (compare current vs earlier observation):\n- Soil moisture unchanged by 0.0% compared to the previous observation (0.0% -> 0.0%).\n- Humidity decreased by 0.4% compared to the previous observation (73.7% -> 73.3%).\n- Temperature unchanged by 0.0C compared to the previous observation (30.7C -> 30.7C).\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [critical] Node offline (no contact for 3 min)\n- [warning] Low soil moisture 0% (water stress)\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nSugarcane Cultivation in India: Agro-Climatic Overview (with Karnataka Focus) — Summary:\nSugarcane (*Saccharum* spp. hybrid) is India's principal commercial/industrial cash crop, cultivated on about 49.18 lakh hectares nationally. India is the world's second-largest producer of both sugarcane and sugar. Cultivation is organized into two broad agro-climatic regions — tropical and sub-tropical — with distinct climatic requirements, planting seasons, and productivity levels. Karnataka falls within the tropical/peninsular zone, where cane yields (85.5 t/ha in 2015-16 data) are markedly higher than the subtropical national average (69.4 t/ha), owing to long sunshine hours, cool nights, and favourable latitude for sugar accumulation.\n\nLand Preparation for Sugarcane Cultivation — Summary:\nBecause sugarcane occupies the field for over a year, thorough land preparation — deep ploughing, repeated harrowing, and periodic cross sub-soiling — is essential to break hard pans, improve water infiltration, and support deep root penetration. IISR recommends specific ploughing and sub-soiling schedules tailored to sugarcane's long crop duration.\n\nSugarcane Cultivation in India: Agro-Climatic Overview (with Karnataka Focus) — Main Information:\n- Sugarcane requires a mean temperature of 28–32°C for optimal growth; temperatures above 45°C reduce tillering, and below 20°C slow growth. Areas with minimum temperature under 5°C are unsuitable.\n- The crop needs 75–120 cm of annual rainfall (if unirrigated) and a long growing season of 10–18 months. Ideal relative humidity is 70–85% during growth and 55–75% during ripening; below 50% humidity during growth is unsuitable.\n- India has two broad sugarcane regions:\n  - **Tropical region** (Maharashtra, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh, Kerala): ~45% of area, 55% of national production; productivity ~80 t/ha.\n\nSugarcane Planting Methods and Advanced Nursery Techniques — Important Recommendations:\n- Match the field-planting method to local rainfall/drainage conditions: flat-bed for low-rainfall/well-drained areas, ridge-and-furrow or trench for higher rainfall/drainage-risk or wind-prone (coastal) areas.\n- Where seed cane is expensive or scarce, adopt STP, cane-node, or bud-chip nursery techniques to cut seed requirement by up to two-thirds while improving germination uniformity and disease-free status of planting material.\n- The FIRB technique is specifically recommended for water-scarce regions practising wheat-sugarcane rotation, as it saves roughly 20% irrigation water without reducing wheat yield.\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: Is soil moisture okay?\n\n(Answer only in Kanglish: Kannada in Latin letters mixed with English. Do not use pure English.)\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"7-8 cm\" (this number is not in Gage's sources); \"Correct timing of irrigation leads to higher yield\" (not in Gage's sources). Sir, Sir, prati irrigation ge 7-8 cm neeru hakbeku, timing correct madidre ...\nFarmer: How is my crop?\n\n(Answer only in Kanglish: Kannada in Latin letters mixed with English. Do not use pure English.)\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"Your current farming practice fits into sustainable sugarcane cultivation practices recommended by experts here in Karnataka\" (not in Gage's sources). Hello Sir, this should help you understand how your ...\nFarmer: Naanu irrigation madbeka?\nGage (earlier, shortened): Idu nodi yes sir, Skip Furrow method inda kelidri, idinda suma 30-40% water save maadoke saakagutte.\n\n# USER QUESTION\nNanna crop hegide?", "farmer": "Nanna crop hegide?"}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "Nanna crop hegide?", "sarvam": "How is my crop?", "groq": "location of sugarcane crop in field"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.8208, "sarvam": 0.8524, "groq": 0.8778}, "retrieved": [{"source": "sugarcane/01_sugarcane_cultivation_overview_india_and_karnataka.md", "section": "Sugarcane Cultivation in India: Agro-Climatic Overview (with Karnataka Focus) — Summary", "score": 0.8778, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "sugarcane/02_land_preparation.md", "section": "Land Preparation for Sugarcane Cultivation — Summary", "score": 0.8653, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "sugarcane/01_sugarcane_cultivation_overview_india_and_karnataka.md", "section": "Sugarcane Cultivation in I